{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>On the day Moshe finished.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 12): ״כַּלַּת״ it is written [without the vav], and it appears to me that in a sefer Torah it is written with a vav, and I say that matters of Torah such as these and similar to these are not handed over to everyone who raises his hand among those who grasp the Torah, for the matter would circle around to heresy and contempt for the honor of teachers. And you should know that the order of our holy Torah — its words and its letters — are counted, refined, hewn from a holy and awesome quarry; they are wondrous to those who understand them, and still more wondrous are those hidden from every eye. And I will awaken you with one grain [of insight]: will you not find that there are letters felt in the pronunciation of the words yet absent from the written text, such as ״יְהוֹשֻׁעַ״ — for in pronunciation there is the sounding of the letter vav between the shin and the ayin, yet it is not in the writing; whereas there is a place where it is not sounded, [yet] it is between the heh and the shin. And this remark will establish the mind of the discerning to recognize that her letters are counted and refined, and it is impossible to add even a single letter, even though it might be needed for pronunciation and would be read aloud even though it is not written, becoming קְרִי וְלֹא כְתִיב (read but not written). And the Torah fixed the vav not in its place to awaken [awareness] in hidden matters. And perhaps from this He has granted you insight — that the intent was to combine three letters from His name, blessed be He, within the name of the righteous one, and to adjoin shin and ayin to allude to ש״ע נְהוֹרִין (the [divine] name ש־ע of illuminations). And had the vav been arranged in its proper place, this knowledge would have been lost, for it would not have been explained. Now, those who understand the arrangements of the letters — they will gain insight from their settings; and they are the ones who say that ״כַּלַּת״ is written and the vav is borrowed there by way of לִשָּׁנָה הַשְׁאָלָה (a borrowed form). And the discerning one will understand that this is the sod of Moshe, with whom is unified and fulfilled ״נְקֵבָה תְּסוֹבֵב גָּבֶר״ (Jeremiah 31:22). And this is the measure of the matter: ״כַּלַּת״ is written — the meaning of this word ״כַּלּוֹת,״ this is its meaning: take from the written text ״כַּלַּת״ in order that you may understand what the word intends, and the letter vav is the aspect of the groom, and it is a hidden sod: a vav within ״כַּלַּת.״ And understand. And this derashah no one expounds from his own mind unless preceded by recognition of the matters in their clarity and truth from the mouth of receivers of the Torah and its sodot and its hints."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The phrase ״ש״ע נְהוֹרִין״ is a kabbalistic reference; I have transliterated it rather than decode it, per the rule that the mystical register is rendered literally. The abbreviation רַזַ״ל is resolved here as ״our Rabbis of blessed memory.״"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:40.252469799Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}